{"type": "thinking", "text": "Now implement all 6. Let me think through each.\n\n**Bowling**: validate. Approach store rolls and frame state. Simpler: store list of rolls and validate on roll; score at end check complete. Need careful handling of 10th frame bonus validation. Could implement roll-by-roll state machine.\n\nLet me design straightforward state machine storing frames.\n\nRolls list approach for score:\n- If frames not complete -> error.\n- Standard score with bonus using rolls.\n\nNeed validation of rolls with 10th frame specifics. I'll maintain `self.rolls` and `self.frames` count? Easier maintain current frame index and roll number.\n\nLet me implement with a list of frames (each list of rolls). On roll:\n- pins must int-ish 0..10.\n- If no frames: start frame 0 with [pins].\n- Track self.frames list, each up to? For frame 10 allow up to 3 rolls.\n\nI'll implement explicit:\n\n```\nclass BowlingGame:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.frames = []  # completed or current frames; frame 1..10\n        self.ended = False\n\n    def roll(self, pins):\n        if self.ended: raise ...\n        if not isinstance(pins,int) or pins<0 or pins>10: raise ValueError(\"invalid roll\")\n        if not self.frames:\n            if pins==10:\n                self._start_new_frame(pins, strike=True)\n            else:\n                self.frames.append([pins])\n            return\n        current = self.frames[-1]\n        idx = len(self.frames)-1  # 0-based\n        if idx < 9:  # frames 1-9\n            # if current frame already complete shouldn't happen because we start new\n            ...\n```\n\nHmm need to handle completion and start next frame. Let's maintain `self.frame = []` list with frame number. Simpler approach with frames list where each frame is list, and current frame is last unless last frame complete and <10 frames in which case start new.\n\nDefine helper `_frame_complete(frame, is_tenth)`:\n- frames 1-9: complete if len>=2 or first==10.\n- 10th: complete if:\n  - first==10: need 3 rolls unless second !=10 and second+third <10? Actually rule: if first strike, two bonus rolls; if first bonus not strike, then total of two bonus can't exceed 10 and second bonus can't be strike. The frame is complete after 3 rolls in any case? Wait if first strike then bonus rolls: if second is not strike, third roll allowed only if second+third <=10, and third rolled even if second+third<10? In canonical ten-pin: after strike, 2 bonus rolls; if first bonus is strike, another bonus; if first bonus non-strike, you get second bonus roll (even if not spare). So yes 3 rolls always after strike. Test `[10,7,1]` final -> score 18, 3 rolls. `[10,7,3]` -> 3 rolls. `[10,6]` then rolling 10 should error (since second bonus after strike first not strike cannot be strike). But `[10,6]` is not complete yet? They test rolling 10 raises. After `[10,6,?]` should allow e.g. 4 (spare) or 0..? Actually total 6+?<=10 and ? !=10. So any 0..4 allowed. Frame complete after 3 rolls.\n  - first !=10: if first+second ==10 (spare): gets one bonus roll, frame complete after 3 rolls. If <10: frame complete after 2 rolls.\n  - Edge: 10th first roll <10, second roll such that sum <10 -> frame over after 2 rolls, cannot roll more. Fine.\n\nFor 10th validation on each roll:\n- roll1: any 0..10.\n- roll2: if frame[0]==10: any 0..10. else frame[0]+pins <=10.\n- roll3: if frame[0]==10:\n   - if frame[1]==10: any 0..10.\n   - else frame[1]+pins <=10 (and pins != 10? Actually if frame[1]+pins <=10 and frame[1]<10, pins<=10 - frame[1] <=9 if frame[1]>=1; if frame[1]==0, pins could be 10 which would be strike. Is that allowed? \"the second bonus rolls after a strike in the last frame cannot be a strike if the first one is not a strike\". Even if first bonus is 0, the second cannot be a strike per spec. Test only 6 then 10. Canonical spec says \"cannot be a strike if the first one is not a strike\" so disallow pins==10 whenever frame[1]!=10. But if frame[1]==0 and pins==10, total 10, still disallow. Let's disallow.\n   - Actually wait if frame[1]==10 then frame[2] any including 10 (test 10,10,6 valid; 10,10,10 score 30 valid).\n  - if frame[0]!=10 and frame[0]+frame[1]==10 (spare): frame[2] any 0..10 (bonus single roll). Test 7,3,10 valid score 20.\n  - if not spare, no third roll allowed (frames complete after 2). Should error.\n\nFrames 1-9 validations:\n- if new frame: pins any 0..10.\n- second roll: first+second <=10.\n- After strike, frame complete with 1 roll; new frame starts next roll.\n\nRoll count/end detection: after frame 10 complete, set ended. Rolling more -> error.\n\nHow to determine when frame complete and whether to start new frame. I'll write:\n\n```\ndef roll(self,pins):\n    if self.ended: raise\n    validate int range\n    if not self.frames:\n        self.frames.append([pins])\n    else:\n        frame = self.frames[-1]\n        if len(self.frames) < 10:\n            # frame 1-9 current\n            if len(frame)==1 and frame[0]==10:\n                # actually should never happen because we would complete and start new; ensure complete logic\n```\n\nBetter: after each roll, mark completed frames and next roll starts a new frame. Use self.current_frame number and self.rolls_in_frame. But last frame.\n\nAlternative design: maintain `self.rolls` and `self.frame_index` and `self.ball`; precompute validation on the fly.\n\nLet me just use frames with a function that on each roll appends to last frame, and before appending, if last frame complete and fewer than 10 frames, append new empty frame. The completed logic for frames 1-9: a frame is complete if len==1 and roll==10, or len==2. Since a strike frame should have only one roll. For 10th use above.\n\n```\ndef roll(self,pins):\n    if self.ended: raise\n    if not isinstance(pins,int) or isinstance(pins,bool) or not 0<=pins<=10: raise ValueError(...)\n    if not self.frames:\n        self.frames.append([])\n    else:\n        last = self.frames[-1]\n        n = len(self.frames)\n        if n < 10:\n            # if last complete, start new\n            if len(last)==2 or (len(last)==1 and last[0]==10):\n                self.frames.append([])\n        else:\n            # tenth frame - if complete, error\n            if self._tenth_complete(last):\n                self.ended=True; raise\n    frame = self.frames[-1]\n    nframes=len(self.frames)\n    if nframes < 10:\n        if len(frame)==0: frame.append(pins)\n        elif len(frame)==1:\n            if frame[0]==10: # shouldn't\n                ...\n            if frame[0]+pins>10: raise\n            frame.append(pins)\n    else:\n        # tenth frame validate\n        if len(frame)==0: frame.append(pins)\n        elif len(frame)==1:\n            if frame[0]==10: frame.append(pins)\n            else:\n                if frame[0]+pins>10: raise\n                frame.append(pins)\n        elif len(frame)==2:\n            if frame[0]==10:\n                if frame[1]==10:\n                    frame.append(pins)\n                else:\n                    if pins==10 or frame[1]+pins>10: raise\n                    frame.append(pins)\n            elif frame[0]+frame[1]==10:\n                frame.append(pins)\n            else:\n                raise\n        else: raise\n    # after adding, if tenth complete => ended\n```\n\n`_tenth_complete`:\n```\nif len(frame)<2: return False\nif frame[0]==10:\n    return len(frame)>=3\nif len(frame)==2 and frame[0]+frame[1]==10:\n    return len(frame)>=3\nreturn len(frame)>=2\n```\nNote if frame[0]==10 and len 2, not complete. If frame[0]<10 len2 non-spare complete. Good.\n\nBut careful: after 9 frames, `self.frames` has 9, appending frame 10. When rolling within tenth, before append we check n<10 false, last=frame10, check _tenth_complete and if complete raise. But when tenth completes, we should set ended on completion rather than wait. Could set ended after append if nframes==10 and _tenth_complete. Then next roll raises. But test \"cannot roll if already has ten frames\" standard zeros: after 10 frames, ended. Good.\n\nHowever there's a subtle bug: for frames 1-9, after a strike we append empty new frame. But if nframes==10? no, only when <10. Good.\n\nScore: check complete: if ended false => error \"game not complete\". But there is a case game complete but ended set. We set ended when frame 10 complete. So score requires ended.\n\nIf no rolls -> error. If only [0,0] -> frames len1 and not complete -> ended false -> error.\n\nCompute score from frames rolls:\n```\ntotal=0\nfor i in range(10):\n  frame=self.frames[i]\n  if i<9:\n    if frame[0]==10:\n      bonus = next two rolls overall\n    elif sum(frame)==10:\n      bonus = next roll\n    else bonus=0\n    total += sum(frame)+bonus\n  else:\n    total += sum(frame)\n```\nEasier flatten rolls with frame boundaries. Just use flat list `self.rolls` alongside frames maybe redundant. I can compute flat from frames: `rolls = [r for f in frames for r in f]`. For i<9, determine bonus by indexing flat. Need positions. Compute cumulative index.\n\n```\nidx=0\nfor i,frame in enumerate(frames):\n  if i==9: total+=sum(frame); break\n  if frame[0]==10:\n    total+=10+rolls[idx+1]+rolls[idx+2]\n  elif sum(frame)==10:\n    total+=10+rolls[idx+2]\n  else: total+=sum(frame)\n  idx+=len(frame)\n```\nWorks given complete.\n\nCould use simpler flat rolls standard scoring; but frames okay.\n\n**Dominoes**: backtracking Hamiltonian cycle with duplicate handling. Input small. Find chain using DFS. Need special empty -> []. Single (1,1) -> [(1,1)] works. Single (1,2) -> None (ends don't match). We can fix starting domino; try each starting domino and orientation, then DFS remaining. n up to maybe 9. Use recursion with used indices.\n\nReturn list of oriented tuples. If n==0 return []. For n>=1, loop start index/orientations. DFS takes current chain end. Ensure final chain[0][0]==chain[-1][1].\n\nTo avoid exponential maybe fine for tests. Could implement start from each index but symmetric; okay.\n\nPotential duplicate values: used indices handles.\n\n```\ndef can_chain(dominoes):\n    if not dominoes: return []\n    n=len(dominoes)\n    used=[False]*n\n    for i in range(n):\n      for a,b in (dominoes[i], (dominoes[i][1],dominoes[i][0])):\n         used[i]=True\n         res=dfs([(a,b)], b, ...)\n         used[i]=False\n         if res: return res\n    return None\n```\nDFS:\n```\nif len(chain)==n:\n    if chain[0][0]==chain[-1][1]: return chain\n    return None\nfor j in range(n):\n  if used[j]: continue\n  x,y=dominoes[j]\n  for u,v in ((x,y),(y,x)):\n    if u==end:\n      used[j]=True\n      r=dfs(chain+[(u,v)],v)\n      used[j]=False\n      if r: return r\nreturn None\n```\nIf start orientation doesn't match final but another start orientation might. Covered.\n\n**Forth**: tokenize input lines; definitions may span lines? Tests each definition in one string. Implement standard approach parse tokens sequentially, with definitions recognized `:` name ... `;`, case-insensitive. Semantics: custom words should capture definitions at definition time (test foo 5; bar foo; foo 6; -> bar gives 5). So store expanded body at definition time using current dictionary. Also recursive self-reference: `: foo 10 ; : foo foo 1 + ; foo` -> when defining new foo, body token `foo` should refer to previous foo (10) -> body [10,1,+] => 11. So expand tokens at definition time substituting current custom words recursively. Numbers remain. Need detect illegal number as word name -> ValueError(\"illegal operation\"). Undefined word in definition? Test not present maybe should raise \"undefined operation\". At definition, if token is custom word, expand its stored body. If token is builtin/number, keep. If unknown -> likely error. But order: custom can override builtins; expansion at definition time uses custom dict if present else builtin. If definition contains itself and no previous, it is undefined -> undefined operation. Fine.\n\nCase-insensitive: normalize tokens lowercase in evaluate and definitions names lower. Builtins lower.\n\nBuiltins can be overridden. When executing, check custom words first, then builtins, then number parse (numbers token). Actually number tokens should always push; but custom word named same as number disallowed. We can at definition check name is numeric -> illegal. Also defining name like `1`. Negative numbers. What about name \":\"? not tested.\n\nTokenization: input_data list of strings; join with \" \" split. Definitions could span but joining all lines with space handles.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```\nclass Forth:\n...\ndef evaluate(input_data):\n    tokens = \" \".join(input_data).split()\n    builtins = {...} # functions operating stack\n    custom = {}\n    # parse and execute\n    i=0\n    def expand(tok, seen):\n        if tok in custom:\n            if tok in seen: ... # shouldn't due definition-time expansion\n            result=[]\n            for t in custom[tok]:\n                result.extend(expand(t, seen|{tok}))\n            return result\n        return [tok]\n```\n\nWait custom word bodies should already be fully expanded except maybe references to words defined later? Forth normally would fail if word undefined at definition. Tests don't require forward refs. At definition time expand each token: if in custom, use expanded body stored (already flat); else if builtin or number, keep; else raise undefined operation. But note builtins overridable: if custom has name, expanded. Good.\n\nSimplify: custom stores fully expanded flat token list. Expand definition:\n```\nbody=[]\nfor t in tokens_between:\n  t=t.lower()\n  if t in custom:\n      body.extend(custom[t])\n  elif t in builtins:\n      body.append(t)\n  elif is_number(t):\n      body.append(t)\n  else:\n      raise ValueError(\"undefined operation\")\ncustom[name]=body\n```\nFor self-reference `: foo foo 1 + ;` where previous foo exists, at time of definition custom[\"foo\"] still old, so expands to old body. Good.\n\nBut what about redefinition in same definition? not needed.\n\nNow lexer/parser of colon definitions. If `:` at position i, find next `;` (after name). name=tokens[i+1]; if missing -> error maybe. If name is number -> illegal operation. Also name `:`? Ignore. Then body tokens i+2..semicolon. If no semicolon -> maybe ValueError undefined? not tested. Token `;` outside definition? probably illegal/undefined. We can raise ValueError(\"undefined operation\").\n\nThen execute remaining tokens sequentially. When encountering custom word, execute its body tokens. Since fully expanded, no nesting issues. But custom body may contain other custom? expanded away. Execute via function.\n\nFor runtime undefined word -> ValueError(\"undefined operation\").\nNumbers: regex `^-?\\d+$`.\nStack underflow: helper pop requiring n, raise StackUnderflowError(\"Insufficient number of items in stack\").\nDivision: integer division truncates toward zero? Forth uses floored? Test `8 3 /` =2. Negative not tested. Use int(a/b) to truncate toward zero (Forth standard is implementation-defined, canonical may expect). Actually canonical forth might test negative division? Not in this test. Use `int(a / b)` maybe floating precision but fine. Or `abs//` sign. We'll use `int(a / b)`.\nDivision by zero: Python raises ZeroDivisionError(\"division by zero\") but test expects \"divide by zero\". Raise ZeroDivisionError(\"divide by zero\") explicitly.\nSubtraction: a b - => a-b where second from top is a. `3 4 -` -> -1.\n\nOrder of operations: for arithmetic pop b then a, push a op b.\n\nBuiltins functions:\n```\ndef binary(op):\n   b=pop(); a=pop(); push(op(a,b))\n```\nNeed underflow check before popping both.\n\nApply token execution: custom body may include builtins; since expanded at definition time to token strings, execute recursively. But if a custom body token is a custom name? We expanded fully, so no. Yet if we expand at definition, fine.\n\nWait issue: `test_user_defined_words_can_use_different_words_with_the_same_name`: `: foo 5 ; : bar foo ; : foo 6 ; bar foo` -> bar body expands foo -> [5]. then foo redefined 6. Execute bar ->5, foo->6 => [5,6]. Good.\n\n`: swap dup ;` defines custom swap as [dup] overriding builtin swap. Execute \"1 swap\" -> custom swap -> dup -> [1,1]. Good.\n\n`: + * ;` -> custom + body [*]. Execute 3 4 + => * =>12. Good.\n\nCase-insensitivity definition name lowercase and tokens lower.\n\nNumbers can appear in definitions. `: countup 1 2 3 ;`.\n\nPotential test `: 1 2 ;` name \"1\" numeric -> illegal. Negative -1 -> illegal. Good.\n\nCould definitions name \";\"? not needed.\n\n**Poker**: Implement rank mapping 2..14. Straight detection: sorted ranks; if consecutive distinct -> high=last; if ranks == [2,3,4,5,14] -> high=5 low ace. Need handle duplicates. Category and tiebreak tuple.\n\nFor each hand, parse cards into ranks and suits. Count ranks. Determine:\n- flush: all suits same.\n- straight high: None if not straight. With 5 cards distinct.\n- categories rank:\n8 straight flush\n7 four kind\n6 full house\n5 flush\n4 straight\n3 three kind\n2 two pair\n1 one pair\n0 high\n\nTiebreak:\n- straight flush/straight: (high,)\n- four: (quad rank, kicker)\n- full house: (trip rank, pair rank)\n- flush/high: sorted ranks descending\n- three: (trip, kickers desc)\n- two pair: (highpair, lowpair, kicker)\n- one pair: (pair, kickers desc)\n\nReturn best hands preserving input order. Compare score tuples; category included. Use max score; include all hands with score == best.\n\n`best_hands` returns original strings (as given) not normalized. Tests expect exact original. Good.\n\n**Zipper**: Need immutable-ish or mutable? Tests chain and to_tree. Straight implementation storing tree and path of directions/parents. Could implement mutable nodes but from_tree should not mutate initial? Tests compare expected, and they don't reuse initial after mutation except maybe. Simpler persistent: each Zipper holds current tree node dict and breadcrumbs list of (parent_node, direction, sibling subtree). to_tree reconstructs ancestors by deep copying? Need avoid aliasing issues.\n\nApproach: use immutable copy-on-write. Represent current node dict; breadcrumbs each contains parent context with left/right that might be original or updated. To reconstruct, copy. Let's design:\n\nZipper fields: `_node` (dict or None for empty? but tree root is dict), `_crumbs` list of dicts:\n- `node` = parent node dict (with value, left, right) maybe mutable.\n- `direction` 'left'/'right'\n- `other` = sibling subtree (the other child)\nWhen descending left: crumb = {parent node copy without target?}. Simplest crumb stores parent value and the sibling subtree, plus direction.\n\nFor left():\n```\nif self._node is None or self._node[\"left\"] is None: return None\ncrumb = {'value': self._node['value'], 'other': self._node['right'], 'dir':'right'}  # meaning current is left, other sibling is right\nreturn Zipper(self._node['left'], self._crumbs+[crumb])\n```\nSo crumb \"dir\" indicates which side the other goes relative to parent. If current is left, other is right. Up reconstruction:\n```\nnode = copy of current node\nfor crumb in reversed(crumbs):\n   if crumb.dir == 'right': parent = {value: crumb.value, left: node, right: crumb.other}\n   else: parent = {value: crumb.value, left: crumb.other, right: node}\n   node=parent\nreturn node\n```\nWait naming: when descending left, current node is left child, sibling is right. Storing `sibling_side='right'`. Then reconstruct parent with left=node (current), right=other. So crumb.side='right' means node goes left. Let's define crumb = (parent_value, child_side, other_subtree), where child_side is side current node was on. Then reconstruct:\n- if child_side=='left': parent left=node, right=other\n- else parent right=node, left=other.\n\nWhen set_value/set_left/set_right, return new Zipper with copied node, same crumbs. No mutation of crumbs; other subtrees may be shared but not mutated. to_tree returns new reconstructed dict; subnodes may share references with input, but tests only equality, and modifications create copies along path. Sharing okay unless test mutates and then expects original unaffected? test set_value then to_tree, no original check. But safer deep copy in to_tree? Could use copy.deepcopy. If we deep copy at from_tree, and set operations copy current node shallowly, other sibling trees shared. Fine.\n\nBut note `set_left` takes subtree dict; store as given (maybe copy deep to isolate). Test just equality. We can use copy.deepcopy on inputs to be safe.\n\n`value` returns self._node[\"value\"].\n\n`set_value(v)` returns new zipper with copied current dict value v.\n\n`set_left(tree)` returns new zipper with copied current node left = deepcopy(tree). If tree None -> None. Similarly right.\n\n`left()` and `right()` return None for dead end. Possibly if node None.\n\n`up()` returns None if no crumbs, else reconstruct parent node and crumbs[:-1], as new Zipper. Note up should preserve modifications: current node used to reconstruct. Good.\n\n`from_tree(tree)` returns Zipper(deepcopy(tree), []).\n\nOne subtlety: `to_tree` for zipper at root returns reconstructed copy (equal). For deep focus rebuilds.\n\n**Pov**: Need implement from_pov and path_to, keeping existing methods. Tree representation label, children list. Equality via __dict__ which sorts children (uses __lt__). Tree may have duplicate labels? likely no. Need from_pov: find node label, reroot. Standard recursive parent orientation.\n\nApproach: find path from root to target and rebuild. Function `_find_path(tree, label)` returns list of nodes from root to target. Then reroot: The expected structure: new root = target node with its original children plus the parent-side subtree appended, where parent subtree is rerooted at parent with target removed.\n\nCould recursively define:\n```\ndef reroot(node, target_label):\n   if node.label == target_label:\n       return Tree(node.label, [deepcopy? original children as-is]) maybe return node children.\n   for i, child in enumerate(node.children):\n       if contains(child,target):\n           newchild = reroot(child,target)\n           # the new root is newchild (target) somewhere\n```\nBetter path-based.\n\nFind path of (node, parent, index) from root to target. Let path = [root, ..., target]. To reroot at target:\n- Start new root = Tree(target.label, [deepcopy of target's original children]).\n- Walk path backwards from target's parent to root:\n  For current node `p` (parent of child along path) and child `c`:\n   Build subtree for p excluding c: Tree(p.label, [children of p except c]) (copy).\n   The rerooted result so far (rooted at target) should have p appended as a child of target root? Wait expected: new root target has its own children at same level as former parent. E.g. parent with x,kid0,kid1; path [parent,x]. new root x children = kid0,kid1 + parent(without x). So p becomes child of target root, not nested? In deeper tree: path [level0, level1, level2, level3, x]. expected x children = [level3 children excluding level2? Let's see tree chain: level0 -> level1 -> level2 -> level3 -> x. Expected x -> level3 -> level2 -> level1 -> level0. So p=level3 becomes child of x, and level3's children should be level2 (constructed), etc. So yes each ancestor becomes nested: new root x gets child p, p gets child grandparent, etc.\n\nAlgorithm:\n```\nresult = Tree(target.label, [copy children of target])\n# path list root..target, path index\ncurrent_child_subtree = result? \nfor idx from len(path)-2 down to 0:\n    p = path[idx]\n    c = path[idx+1]\n    # build p subtree excluding c:\n    p_new = Tree(p.label, [copy of child for child in p.children if child is not c (by identity/index)])\n    # attach p_new to result? Actually attach as child of c_new (the node in result corresponding to c)\n```\nSince result root corresponds target, then first p=parent attaches to result (which is target). Next p=grandparent should attach to the node representing parent inside result. We need track `attach_point`: initially result; after creating p_new, attach p_new to attach_point.children; then set attach_point = p_new for next iteration.\n\nWait orientation: at idx=len-2, p=parent, c=target. p_new excludes target. attach to result root (target). attach_point=p_new. Next idx p=grandparent, c=parent. p_new excludes parent's subtree. Attach p_new to attach_point (the parent_new, corresponding to c). Yes. This yields chain.\n\nBut careful: when building p_new, p.children excluding c. Need deep copy of siblings. Also preserve original children ordering. Expected test for complex tree: x children originally [kid-0,kid-1]; expected [kid-1,kid-0] because equality sorts children, so order irrelevant. They also sort in __dict__. Parent p children excluding x: sibling-0, sibling-1 (in original order). Then grandparent excluding parent: uncle. So result x children = kid0,kid1,parent; parent children sibling0,sibling1,grandparent; grandparent children uncle. matches.\n\nBut wait when new root target has original children and also appended parent. Good.\n\nNeed find path with node references. Implement:\n\n```\ndef _path(self,label):\n    if self.label==label: return [self]\n    for child in self.children:\n        p=child._path(label)\n        if p: return [self]+p\n    return None\n```\nBut this returns path of actual original nodes. Building p_new children excluding c: compare child is not c. Fine.\n\nCopy helper `_copy_tree(node)` returns new Tree(node.label, [_copy_tree(c) for c in node.children]). To avoid mutating original.\n\nfrom_pov:\n```\npath=self._path(from_node)\nif path is None: raise ValueError(\"Tree could not be reoriented\")\ntarget=path[-1]\nresult=Tree(target.label, [_copy_tree(c) for c in target.children])\nattach=result\nfor idx in range(len(path)-2,-1,-1):\n   p=path[idx]; c=path[idx+1]\n   new_p=Tree(p.label, [_copy_tree(child) for child in p.children if child is not c])\n   attach.children.append(new_p)\n   attach=new_p\nreturn result\n```\nHmm when target==root path length1 result copy root children. good.\n\npath_to(from_node,to_node):\n- if from not exists -> ValueError(\"Tree could not be reoriented\")\n- if to not exists -> ValueError(\"No path found\")\n- Path between nodes in undirected tree. Could reroot at from and find path to to. Simpler find paths from root to each; LCA. Or compute via from_pov geometry. Use root paths:\n```\np1=_path(from_node); p2=_path(to_node)\nif p1 is None: raise ValueError(\"Tree could not be reoriented\")\nif p2 is None: raise ValueError(\"No path found\")\n# find LCA\ni=0 while i<min(len) and p1[i] is p2[i]: i++\nlca_index=i-1\npath = [labels from from up to lca] + [labels from lca child down to to]\n= [n.label for n in reversed(p1[lca_index:])] + [n.label for n in p2[lca_index+1:]]\n```\nWait reversed(p1[lca_index:]) includes lca -> from. Then p2[lca_index+1:] gives nodes after lca to to. Correct. Example x to cousin-1: p1 root grandparent,parent,x; p2 grandparent,uncle,cousin1. lca index0 grandparent. [x,parent,grandparent] + [uncle,cousin1] -> expected. Good.\nSource==destination -> [label]. Fine.\nNeed error order: source checked before destination. Test source nonexistent dest x -> \"Tree could not be reoriented\"; dest nonexistent -> \"No path found\". Do source first.\n\nPotential issue `Tree.__dict__` sorts children via __lt__; our new Tree objects okay.\n\nAlso from_pov when target is direct child etc.\n\nNow implement files. Use write. Since skeleton read. Let me write each."}
{"type": "tool_call", "callId": "call_00_9AbewPMVepAMPFQXEQhk4975", "tool": "write", "input": {"file_path": "/Users/b612-lp/bench-workspaces/r1031/dsh/2/exercism-py-6/inputs/exercises/bowling/bowling.py", "content": "class BowlingGame:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.frames = []\n        self.ended = False\n\n    def _tenth_complete(self, frame):\n        if len(frame) < 2:\n            return False\n        if frame[0] == 10:\n            return len(frame) >= 3\n        if len(frame) == 2 and frame[0] + frame[1] == 10:\n            return False\n        return len(frame) >= 2\n\n    def roll(self, pins):\n        if self.ended:\n            raise ValueError(\"game is over\")\n        if not isinstance(pins, int) or isinstance(pins, bool):\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid roll\")\n        if pins < 0 or pins > 10:\n            raise ValueError(\"invalid roll\")\n\n        if not self.frames:\n            self.frames.append([])\n        else:\n            last = self.frames[-1]\n            if len(self.frames) < 10:\n                if len(last) == 2 or (len(last) == 1 and last[0] == 10):\n                    self.frames.append([])\n            elif self._tenth_complete(last):\n                self.ended = True\n                raise ValueError(\"game is over\")\n\n        frame = self.frames[-1]\n        if len(self.frames) < 10:\n            if not frame:\n                frame.append(pins)\n            else:\n                if frame[0] + pins > 10:\n                    raise ValueError(\"frame score cannot exceed 10\")\n                frame.append(pins)\n        else:\n            if not frame:\n                frame.append(pins)\n            elif len(frame) == 1:\n                if frame[0] == 10 or frame[0] + pins <= 10:\n                    frame.append(pins)\n                else:\n                    raise ValueError(\"frame score cannot exceed 10\")\n            elif len(frame) == 2:\n                if frame[0] == 10:\n                    if frame[1] == 10:\n                        frame.append(pins)\n                    elif pins == 10 or frame[1] + pins > 10:\n                        raise ValueError(\"invalid bonus roll\")\n                    else:\n                        frame.append(pins)\n                elif frame[0] + frame[1] == 10:\n                    frame.append(pins)\n                else:\n                    raise ValueError(\"frame is complete\")\n            else:\n                raise ValueError(\"frame is complete\")\n\n        if len(self.frames) == 10 and self._tenth_complete(self.frames[-1]):\n            self.ended = True\n\n    def score(self):\n        if not self.ended:\n            raise ValueError(\"game is not complete\")\n\n        rolls = [roll for frame in self.frames for roll in frame]\n        total = 0\n        index = 0\n        for i, frame in enumerate(self.frames):\n            if i == 9:\n                total += sum(frame)\n                break\n            if frame[0] == 10:\n                total += 10 + rolls[index + 1] + rolls[index + 2]\n            elif sum(frame) == 10:\n                total += 10 + rolls[index + 2]\n            else:\n                total += sum(frame)\n            index += len(frame)\n        return total\n"}}
